From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: AltarsCN <337293323+Altars3668@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 27 Sep 2026 16:46:40 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] d2d1: draw round, bevel and miter-or-bevel line joins

Every join was drawn as a miter.  Joins are still built as miters, and
are now cut back in the shaders: the outline vertex shader brings the tip
of a join, and the inner point opposite it, back to the bevel line when
the miter is longer than the limit (always, for a bevel), and for round
joins hands the pixel shader the offset from the point joined at, which
drops what lies further than half a stroke width from it.  The sides of a
segment lie on that circle, and the outer edges of a miter touch it where
they leave the sides, so what is left of the miter is the round join.

A point the path goes straight on through made a "join" reaching 25 units
along the next segment, past its end if it was shorter; nothing is joined
there now.

A miter longer than the limit is still drawn in full, where D2D cuts it
off, and a join where the path turns right back still reaches the same 25
units past the point for bevels.  The tests follow from the geometry and
were not run on Windows; each pixel checked lies wholly on one side of
every edge, so antialiasing does not change it.  d2d1: 18627 tests, 0 failures, on GL and Vulkan.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
(cherry picked from commit 89a0f91bf6d4c070c02b0df1d7388c92821925bc)

